<p>A compact white cauliflower can develop beige, brown or almost black specks after a few days. Sometimes these are simply <strong>surface browning and loss of quality</strong>; sometimes they are part of spoilage that is no longer worth trimming. The useful approach is to assess the whole head rather than judging colour alone.</p>
<p>A cauliflower head consists of thousands of tightly packed floral structures. That shape traps moisture, bruises easily and creates many small spaces where early deterioration can hide. Learning to distinguish a dry surface blemish from soft, wet or visibly mouldy tissue is therefore important.</p>
<h2>Small dry brown specks: the common situation</h2>
<p>As cauliflower ages, some surfaces can oxidise and develop brown specks. Similar marks can appear after rubbing or pressure. If they are <strong>small, dry and superficial</strong>, the head remains firm and there is no abnormal smell, the issue is normally one of quality rather than widespread rot.</p>
<p>In that situation the affected areas can be trimmed before cooking. The remainder may still taste perfectly good, although a cauliflower showing increasing browning has lost freshness and should be used soon.</p>
<h2>Why cauliflower darkens</h2>
<p>Cutting, bruising, dehydration and ageing damage plant cells. Enzymes and phenolic compounds can then interact with oxygen and produce darker pigments. This type of browning occurs in many vegetables after tissue injury.</p>
<p>The rate depends on variety, temperature, time since harvest, humidity and handling. A few dark marks do not automatically mean disease or decay.</p>
<h2>When the black spot no longer looks dry</h2>
<p>The assessment changes if an area is <strong>wet, sunken, soft or slimy</strong>. Those signs indicate tissue breakdown and may accompany microbial growth. An unpleasant smell, leaking fluid or deterioration spreading through many florets are also reasons not to use the head.</p>
<p>When a cauliflower is extensively spoiled, separating individual apparently good florets is not a sensible strategy. Its compact branching structure makes the true extent of deterioration difficult to judge.</p>
<h2>What if it looks like mould?</h2>
<p>A flat brown mark is different from fuzzy, cottony or powdery growth. If you see <strong>visible mould</strong>, especially between florets or in several areas, discard the cauliflower. Do not put it close to your face to smell it or shake spores around the kitchen.</p>
<p>General food-safety guidance sometimes permits trimming a small mould spot from certain very firm vegetables, but a branched cauliflower head is harder to assess than a solid carrot or pepper. If mould has entered between florets, the conservative household choice is to discard it.</p>
<h2>Marks on the outer leaves</h2>
<p>Protective leaves often age before the head itself. Dry edges, yellowing or minor damage on those leaves do not necessarily mean the cauliflower is bad. Remove them and inspect the edible head underneath.</p>
<p>If the leaves are slimy, smell unpleasant or are wet and decaying around the base, inspect the stem carefully because deterioration can progress inward.</p>
<h2>A slightly yellow cauliflower is not automatically spoiled</h2>
<p>Perfect white is a commercial ideal, not the only acceptable colour. Some cultivars are naturally ivory and ageing can introduce a slight cream tone. Firmness, smell and tissue condition are more informative than a small colour change.</p>
<h2>What texture should good cauliflower have?</h2>
<p>The florets should be compact and firm. With age they can loosen and lose turgor. Slight softening means lower freshness; gelatinous, watery or slimy tissue is a much stronger warning sign.</p>
<p>Check the stem as well. It should remain solid. Deep soft or wet areas at the base deserve caution.</p>
<h2>Can you simply cut off brown spots?</h2>
<p>Yes, when they are small, <strong>dry and superficial</strong> and everything else looks normal. Use a clean knife, remove the altered tissue with a margin and cook the cauliflower promptly.</p>
<p>If you would need to cut away a large proportion of the head to make it look acceptable, the cauliflower is already too aged or deteriorated to be worth keeping.</p>
<h2>How to store cauliflower to slow browning</h2>
<p>Cauliflower benefits from refrigeration. Keep it cold and protected from dehydration without allowing free condensation to sit on the surface. Do not wash it before storage if that means leaving it wet for several days; washing shortly before use is usually easier.</p>
<p>If packaging accumulates water, reduce trapped moisture. The aim is to preserve moisture inside the plant tissue, not create a wet surface.</p>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-store-vegetables-what-goes-in-the-fridge-and-what-does-not/">storing vegetables properly</a> explains why cauliflower and broccoli should normally be prioritised early in a weekly shop.</p>
<h2>Can you freeze it before it deteriorates?</h2>
<p>Yes, if it is still in good condition. Freezing does not rescue spoiled produce, but it is useful when you know you cannot cook a sound cauliflower in time. Blanching before freezing is commonly used to protect quality.</p>
<h2>A quick decision rule</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Small dry surface specks + firm head:</strong> trim and use soon.</li>
<li><strong>Extensive browning but no rot:</strong> lower quality; judge whether enough good cauliflower remains.</li>
<li><strong>Soft, wet, slimy or foul-smelling area:</strong> discard.</li>
<li><strong>Visible mould between florets:</strong> discard the head.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Are brown spots on cauliflower fungus?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Many dry specks come from oxidation, bruising or ageing. Mould generally has a fuzzy or powdery structure rather than being only a flat colour change.</p>
<h3>Can I eat cauliflower after cutting off a few spots?</h3>
<p>Yes, when the spots are small and dry and the head remains firm with no abnormal smell or soft tissue. Trim the affected area generously and use the cauliflower soon.</p>
<h3>Does boiling make a rotten cauliflower safe?</h3>
<p>No. Cooking should not be used as a method for rescuing clearly spoiled vegetables. Assess and trim produce before cooking.</p>
<h3>Why do spots appear so quickly?</h3>
<p>Cauliflower is relatively perishable. Age, bruising, moisture and temperature can accelerate browning and spoilage, so it is best used early in the shopping cycle.</p>
